# Highly Efficient Fullerene-Free Polymer Solar Cells Fabricated with Polythiophene Derivative

## abstract
A highly efficient fullerene-free polymer solar cell (PSC) based on PDCBT, a polythiophene derivative substituted with alkoxycarbonyl, achieves an impressive power conversion efficiency of 10.16%, which is the best result in PSCs based on polythiophene derivatives to date. In comparison with a poly(3-hexylthiophene):ITIC-based device, the photovoltaic and morphological properties of the PDCBT:ITIC-based device are carefully investigated and interpreted.

Polythiophene (PT) and its derivatives have been intensively studied for application in organic field-effect transistors (OFETs)[1] and organic photovoltaics (OPVs).[2] Among these polymers, the regioregular poly(3-hexylthiophene) (P3HT) has been the most prominent photovoltaic donor material and played a critical role in the development of OPVs with the bulk heterojunction (BHJ) structure[3] due to its high crystallinity, thermal stability, and good hole mobility. By optimizing the device fabrication conditions, polymer solar cells (PSCs) based on P3HT as an electron-donor material have achieved the power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) of ${\approx}5\%$ and $\approx7\%$ where [6,6]-phenyl C71 butyric acid methyl ester $(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM})^{[4]}$ and indene-C70 bisadduct $(\mathrm{IC}_{70}\mathrm{BA})^{[5]}$ were employed as electronacceptor materials, respectively. However, P3HT has a quite large bandgap, which restrains its light absorption and limits the short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ in PSC devices. Besides, P3HT possesses an ionization potential (IP) of $\approx-5.06\ \mathrm{eV}$ (see Figure 1b) and hence it is difficult to obtain a higher open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ , because the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of PSCs is directly proportional to the difference between the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) of the acceptor and the highest unoccupied molecular orbital (HOMO) of the donor.[6] Thus, in order to further improve the performance of PSCs, great efforts have been made to design new PT derivatives to complement weak points of P3HT and realize better efficiency.[6–10] However, PCEs of the PSCs based on PTs are still much lower than the state-of-the-art PCEs obtained from the PSCs using low bandgap polymers as donor and $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ as acceptor, i.e., $\mathrm{PCE}>10\%$ .[11–15]
During last decades, fullerene derivatives, i.e., $\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM}$ and $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ , are the most widely investigated and successful materials in high-performance PSCs.[16–20] However, since these two fullerene derivatives and PTs have relatively narrow absorption band in the visible spectrum, photovoltaic performance of the PT:fullerene-based PSCs are strongly limited.[21] To address this issue, non-fullerene small-molecular acceptors with low bandgap become competitive alternatives to fullerene derivatives.[22,23] Recently, Zhan and co-workers synthesized a novel promising small-molecular acceptor, 3,9-bis(2-methylene-(3-(1,1-dicyanomethylene)-indanone)-5,5,11,11-tetrakis(4- hexylphenyl)-dithieno $[2,3-d:2^{\prime},3^{\prime}-d^{\prime}]$ -s-indaceno[1,2-b:5,6-b′]dithiophene (ITIC, see Figure 1a) and achieved a PCE of $6.8\%$ by combination with PTB7-Th, which is close to the efficiency of PTB7-Th: $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ devices.[24] It is worthy to note, the absorption spectrum of ITIC overlaps with that of PTB7-Th, so that the device showed lower external quantum efficiency (EQE) in a short-wavelength region. To overcome this problem, large bandgap polymers, such as PT derivatives, can be the ideal alternatives due to their complementary absorption in short-wavelength region. However, very limited works have been reported to focus on the applications of P3HT or other PT derivatives in fullerene-free PSCs, showing relatively poor PCEs.[25–29] Therefore, to make highly efficient fullerene-free PSCs by using PT derivatives as donor is an interesting but challenging topic.
Recently, we demonstrate a new alkoxycarbonyl-functionalized PT derivative, poly[ $^{(4,4^{\prime}}$ -bis(2-butyloctoxycarbonyl- $^{\cdot}[2,2^{\prime}$ - bithiophene]-5,5-diyl)-alt- $\scriptstyle(2,2^{\prime}$ -bithiophene- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -diyl)][10] (PDCBT, see Figure 1a). When $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ was used as electron acceptor, in comparison with the PSC based on P3HT, the PDCBT-based PSCs exhibited similar $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and fill factor (FF) but higher $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and hence showed an improved PCE of $7.0\%$ , because PDCBT has similar optical bandgap but deeper HOMO level. In recent works, ITIC and the likes exhibited promising photovoltaic properties when blended with some photovoltaic polymers with conjugated side chains.[24,30,31] In this work, we tried to combine ITIC with P3HT and PDCBT to fabricate fullerene-free PSCs, respectively. It is very surprising to see that although these two polymers are all ideal donor materials for the PSCs using $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ as acceptor, the blends of P3HT:ITIC and PDCBT:ITIC exhibited very different photovoltaic properties. The morphological studies by atomic force microscopy (AFM), transmission electron microscopy (TEM), and grazing incident wide-angle X-ray scattering (GIWAXS) exhibited that these two polymers had distinctly different aggregation structures in neat films and also in blend films with ITIC. The analysis of the current density–voltage characteristics of the PSCs revealed that the P3HT:ITIC-based device exhibited severe bimolecular recombination compared to the PDCBT:ITIC-based device. As a result, for the P3HT:ITIC-based PSCs, poor PCE of $1.25\%$ was obtained, but the PDCBT:ITIC-based PSCs demonstrated a remarkable efficiency of $10.16\%$ , which is among the top values in the field of PSCs, especially for the fullerene-free PSCs. Considering that PTs are a type of classic conjugated polymers and can be made by low-cost procedures but their positions in the PSC field are being replaced by the high-cost low-bandgap polymers due to their limited photovoltaic performance in device, our results clearly demonstrate that outstanding PCEs can be achieved by using a PT derivative as donor and a non-fullerene acceptor. Moreover, our results also indicate that when blended with the non-fullerene acceptor, the PTs that are well compatible with fullerene acceptors may exhibit extremely different photovoltaic properties in fullerene-free PSCs, and hence their chemical structures may need to be modified by some suitable functional groups like the alkoxycarbonyl as demonstrated in this work.
The cyclic voltammogram measurement for materials was carried out, as shown in Figure 1b, P3HT and PDCBT possess similar optical bandgaps, but the ionization potential (IP, approximate HOMO) and the electron affinity (EA, approximate LUMO) of PDCBT are significantly down-shifted. The absorption spectra of the two polymers are complementary with that of ITIC (see Figure 1c), and taking ITIC as acceptor, the energy offsets of $\mathrm{HOMO}_{\mathrm{donor}}\mathrm{-HOMO}_{\mathrm{acceptor}}$ and the LUMO$\scriptstyle\mathrm{donor}^{\mathrm{-LUMO_{\mathrm{acceptor}}}}$ for both two polymers are sufficient for driving the exciton dissociation process.[21] The solid films of the two polymers processed by high boiling point solvents like chlorobenzene or $\textit{o}$ -dichlorobenzene showed high crystallinities.[10,32–35] However, when the low boiling chloroform (good solvent for the two polymers) was used as a processing solvent, the P3HT and PDCBT exhibited very different crystalline structures in film. As demonstrated in the GIWAXS measurements (Figure 2 and Figure S1, Supporting Information), the PDCBT as-spun film processed by chloroform shows the pronounced (100), (200), (300), and (400) diffraction peaks located at 0.29, 0.59, 0.9, and $1.2\mathrm{\mathring{A}}^{-1}$ in the out-of-plane (OOP) direction, respectively, arising from the long-range ordered laminar packing with a $d$ -spacing of $21.7\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . In addition, the pristine PDCBT film shows a more intensive diffraction at ${\approx}1.74\mathrm{\mathring{A}}^{-1}$ in in-plane (IP) direction than that in OOP direction, ascribing to the (010) $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking with $d$ -spacing of 3.61 Å. The as-cast P3HT film processed by chloroform exhibits the clear (100) and weak (200) and (300) diffractions for a laminar packing with $d$ -spacing of ${\approx}19.2\$ Å and an intensive (010) $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking diffraction at $1.61\mathring\mathrm{A}^{-1}$ ( $d$ -spacing of $3.90\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ) in the OOP direction. The result indicates that, with chloroform processing, PDCBT tends to form an edge-on orientation but P3HT is prone to form a face-on orientation. Moreover, as chloroform evaporates very quickly during spin-coating, PDCBT exhibits more ordered crystalline structure with more compact $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking (3.61 Å) than P3HT $(3.90\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ in short processing time. After annealing treatment at $160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ , the diffraction signals of the two films could be significantly enhanced, it means the crystallinities and the chain packing orientations in the two films can be efficiently improved by thermal annealing (see Figure 2b,d). What is more, we also did GIWAXS measurements for the neat
ITIC films processed from chloroform solution before and after thermal treatment, and as shown in Figure 2e,f, ITIC is found to be an amorphous material with a face-on $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking with $d$ -spacing of $4.{\overset{\textstyle-}{3}}0{\overset{\circ}{\mathrm{A}}}.$ and little change was found after thermal annealing.
Table S1 (Supporting Information) summarizes the photovoltaic characteristics for the devices with the optimal D/A weight ratio (1:1) under AM 1.5G standard solar spectrum $(100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ illumination, the PDCBT:ITIC device showed a PCE of $9.5\%$ , which is much higher than that of the P3HT:ITIC device $(\mathrm{PCE}=0.98\%)$ ). After thermal annealing at $160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ , the PCE of PDCBT:ITIC-based device was improved to $10.16\%$ , accompanying with $V_{\mathrm{oc}}=0.94~\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{:}}$ , $J_{\mathrm{sc}}=16.50~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and $\mathrm{FF}=65.67\%$ (shown in Figure 3a and Table 1). For the P3HT:ITIC-based device, thermal annealing brought a little decrease in FF and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ but is helpful for improving $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ . However, the devices based on P3HT:ITIC still showed poor PCEs of ${\approx}1\%$ by various treatments. As shown in Figure 3b, the P3HT:ITIC-based device showed very low EQEs in the whole response range from 300 to ${800}\mathrm{nm}$ , while for the PDCBT:ITICbased PSC, the EQE values exceed $60\%$ in the most absorption region and even surpassed $70\%$ in the range of $550{-}700~\mathrm{nm}$ .
The $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ calculated by the integration of the EQE spectrum is $16.05\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , which agrees well with the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ value obtained from the $J{-}V$ measurement.
As mentioned above, photovoltaic performance of the P3HT-based device was very different with that of the PDCBTbased PSCs. At first, to reveal the differences in charge carrier dynamics of devices, the charge generation and extraction characteristics were studied. Figure 3c shows the photocurrent density $(J_{\mathrm{ph}})$ versus the effective voltage $(V_{\mathrm{eff}})$ of devices. $J_{\mathrm{ph}}$ is defined as $J_{\mathrm{ph}}=J_{\mathrm{Light}}-J_{\mathrm{D}}$ , where $J_{\mathrm{Light}}$ and $J_{\mathrm{D}}$ represent for the photocurrent densities under light illumination and in the dark, respectively. Meanwhile, $V_{\mathrm{eff}}=V_{0}-V_{\mathrm{a}},$ where $V_{0}$ is
