the voltage when $J_{\mathrm{ph}}$ equals zero and $V_{\mathrm{a}}$ is the applied voltage. Since $V_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is determined by the internal electric field in BHJ, influencing the charge carriers transport and extraction. We observed that the $J_{\mathrm{ph}}$ linearly increased with increasing the voltage at a low voltage $(V_{\mathrm{eff}})$ region and then became saturated at a sufficiently high value of $V_{\mathrm{eff}}.$ . To compare the exciton dissociation and bimolecular recombination in two PSCs, we calculated the values of $G_{\mathrm{max}}$ to estimate the charge generation characteristics.[34] By assuming all of the photogenerated excitons dissociate and contribute to the current generation in the saturated region, $G_{\mathrm{max}}$ is defined as $G_{\mathrm{max}}={\frac{J_{\mathrm{sat}}}{q L}}$ = Jsat , where Jsat is the saturation photocurrent density, $\boldsymbol{q}$ is the electron charge, and $L$ is the thickness of the active layer. Hence, the $G_{\mathrm{max}}$ and $J_{\mathrm{sat}}$ of the PDCBT:ITIC-based device $(1.058\times10^{28}\mathrm{m}^{-3}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , $J_{\mathrm{sat}}=$ $196.78\mathrm{~A~m}^{-2})$ ) are more than two times higher than those for the P3HT:ITIC-based device $(4.29~\times~10^{27}~\mathrm{~m}^{-3}~\mathrm{~s}^{-1}$ , $J_{\mathrm{sat}}~=$ $69.29\mathrm{~A~m}^{-2}$ . As shown in Figure 3b, although the P3HT:ITIC blend film shows the weaker optical density than the PDCBT:ITIC blend film in the range of $550{-}800~\mathrm{nm}$ , the difference in optical density is still smaller to explain the difference in their $G_{\mathrm{max}}$ and $J_{\mathrm{sat}}$ values.
To unravel the differences in recombination mechanism between these two devices, we first employed PL quenching measurement to investigate the geminate recombination in the blend films of PDCBT:ITIC and P3HT:ITIC, compared to the pristine films of PDCBT and P3HT (shown in Figure 2d). By exciting at the wavelength of $550\ \mathrm{nm}$ , although the PL quenching in PDCBT:ITIC is a little better than that in P3HT:ITIC, it can be obviously found that the PL quenching of either PDCBT:ITIC or P3HT:ITIC is almost complete, which means charge transfer must be very efficient and the geminate recombination is not serious for both systems.
We also make the semilogarithmic plot of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ as a function of the light intensity, which shows a linear relationship with a slope of $k T/q$ (shown in Figure 3e). If the Shockley−Read−Hall (SRH) recombination is involved, a stronger dependence of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ on the light intensity with a slope greater than $k T/q$ will be observed.[40,41] As shown below, the dependence of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ on the light intensity for P3HT:ITIC blend is measured with a slope of $1.52\mathrm{KT/e}$ , compared to that of $1.14\mathrm{KT/e}$ for PDCBT:ITIC blend. It suggests that more SRH recombination involves under open-circuit condition in P3HT:ITIC than PDCBT:ITIC, and bimolecular recombination should be the main charge recombination mechanism in PDCBT:ITIC blend. In this case, bimolecular recombination should be the main charge recombination mechanism. Then, the exciton dissociation probabilities $\left[P(E,T)\right]$ were also estimated for comparison. The P[(E,T)] value near $100\%$ means that the geminate recombination loss of the charge-transfer exciton is negligible. Since only a portion of photo-generated excitons are able to dissociate into charge carriers at interface, the $J_{\mathrm{ph}}$ of device could be determined by $J_{\mathrm{ph}}=q G_{\mathrm{max}}P[(E,T)]L.$ Under a short-circuit condition $(J_{\mathrm{ph}}=J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ at $V_{\mathrm{a}}=0~\mathrm{V})$ ), the value of $P[(E,T)]$ for PDCBT:ITIC-based device was estimated to be as high as $92\%$ compared to that $(60\%)$ of P3HT:ITIC-based device. It is strongly suggested that a large portion of excitons recombine in P3HT:ITIC before charge separation. This may come from different morphology of two blend systems (detailed morphological analyses will follow).
The light-intensity dependent $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ measurement was also examined to investigate the nongeminate bimolecular recombination losses in the devices (Figure 3d). In general, the relationship between $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and incident light intensity can be described as $\dot{J}_{\mathrm{sc}}\propto P_{\mathrm{light}}^{S}$ . S should be equal to 1 if all dissociated free carriers are collected at the corresponding electrodes without charge recombination, while $S<1$ indicates the presence of some extent of bimolecular recombination.[35–37] Both of the devices herein showed a linear dependence of current density on the light intensity in logarithmic coordinates with a slope of 0.99 for the PDCBT:ITIC device and 0.91 for the P3HT:ITICbased device. It indicates that the former can sweep out charge carriers more efficiently with negligible bimolecular recombination compared to that of the latter.
In addition, space charge limited current measurement $\mathrm{(SCLC)^{[42]}}$ was employed to investigate the vertical charge mobility of the devices (shown in Figure S6, Supporting Information). Although the two blends showed comparable hole mobilities, i.e., $2.4\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ for PDCBT:ITIC versus $1.4\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ for P3HT:ITIC, the electron mobility of the PDCBT:ITIC blend $(1.63\times10^{-3}\mathrm{\cm}^{2}\mathrm{\V}^{-1}\mathrm{\s}^{-1})$ is one order of magnitude greater than that of the P3HT:ITIC blend $(1.68\times10^{-4}\mathrm{{\cm}^{2}\ V^{-1}\ s^{-1})}$ . We further investigate the charge mobilities of blend films in different active layer thickness (shown in Figure S7 and Table S5, Supporting Information). Compared to P3HT:ITIC, PDCBT:ITIC exhibits comparable hole mobility but higher electron mobility.
GIWAXS measurements were also carried out to study the molecular packing properties in the two blend films that processed under the optimal device fabrication condition. As shown in Figure 4 and Figure S1 (Supporting Information), the PDCBT:ITIC blend film shows the laminar packing diffraction peaks at 0.29, 0.59, and $0.9\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}$ in the OOP direction, which are mainly attributed to the ordered PDCBT (same with pristine PDCBT film). A strong $\pi{-}\pi$ diffraction at $1.74\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}$ in the OOP direction and much weaker $\pi{-}\pi$ diffraction at $1.74\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}^{1}$ were measured in the IP direction, implying that most of the edge-on $\pi{-}\pi$ packing in the neat PDCBT film is changed into the face-on oriented $\pi{-}\pi$ packing structure in the blend with ITIC. This is beneficial for efficient charge transport (in the PSC device) in the vertical direction. We also observed that a big hump-shaped diffraction in the range of $1.10{-}1.50\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}$ suggesting randomly oriented $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking of ITIC. After thermal annealing, the diffraction peaks became more sharp and pronounced. The peaks at $0.43{\bar{\mathrm{~A}}}^{-1}$ (100)′ in the IP direction and $1.46\ \mathring\mathrm{\A}^{-1}$ (010)′ in the OOP direction are attributed to the enhanced ordering of ITIC. On the other hand, the ascasted P3HT:ITIC blend shows a weak (100) diffraction peak and a broad diffraction pattern $(1.2\mathrm{-}1.9\mathrm{~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1})$ ). Upon thermal treatments, the lamellar scattering peaks up to (300) appeared in the OOP direction and the strong laminar peak at $\bar{0.42\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}}$ (in the IP direction) was also measured from ordered ITIC in the annealed P3HT:ITIC. Two strong $\pi{-}\pi$ diffractions at 1.46 and1.61 $\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}$ (from ITIC and P3HT domains, respectively) were clearly observed. Interestingly, in the annealed P3HT:ITIC blend film, the (010)′ $\pi{-}\pi$ diffraction of ITIC in the OOP direction is much stronger than that in the annealed neat ITIC film (see Figure 2f) while ITIC shows a hump-shaped (010)′ diffraction ring in the annealed PDCBT:ITIC blend film, implying that
ITIC tends to keep disordered rather than be aligned with the orientation of the donor in PDCBT:ITIC blend. This different morphology can account for different charge generation and transport dynamics in two blend devices. Furthermore, AFM and TEM measurements were used to investigate the micromorphologies of the blend films. Thermal annealing was found to result in increased surface roughness without obvious largescale aggregation (see Figures S8 and S9, Supporting Information). Impressively, the PDCBT:ITIC film formed into fibrillar networks after thermal treatment, which is favorable for exciton dissociation and charge transport, while the P3HT:ITIC film shows a uniform surface morphology (see Figure 4e,f).
Taking into account all of the experimental data, we explain an overall picture as follows: the molecular energy level alignments and optical absorption properties of both blends can meet the requirements for realizing efficient PSCs, so the great difference in photovoltaic properties should be interpreted in terms of blend morphologies. With comparison to P3HT:ITIC, the PDCBT:ITIC device after thermal annealing shows (1) higher crystalline and well-distributed nano-fibrillar structures (with face-on stacking) in the blend film, (2) comparable hole mobility but higher electrons mobility, and (3) efficient charge generation and extraction with little geminate and nongeminate charge recombinations, thus shows $\approx10\%$ PCE. On the contrary, the as-casted P3HT:ITIC device shows an almost amorphous morphology and the crystalline interchain ordering in P3HT decreased substantially even with thermal annealing by blending with ITIC (see Figure 4d), where a large portion of excitons might recombine geminately and the separated charge carriers also suffer from bimolecular recombination. The highly crystalline nature in PDCBT may be related to the alkoxycarbonyl substituents on PDCBT, which may induce intra- and inter-chain dipole–dipole interactions via $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ (carbonyl)⋅⋅⋅S(thienyl).[41,43] It is noteworthy to mention that the edge-on orientation of pristine PDCBT was changed to face-on tendency upon blending with ITIC, indicating a certain kind of favorable interactions between PDCBT and ITIC, probably due to dipole–dipole interactions of the polar alkoxycarbonyl substituents and dicyanomethyleneindanone moieties in ITIC. Further investigation on the detailed intermolecular interaction between these polythiophene derivatives and ITIC, and charge dynamics will be carried out as a separate work.
In conclusion, we successfully demonstrate a highly efficient fullerene-free PSC based on the PT derivative, which achieves an impressive efficiency as high as $10.16\%$ , and this result is also the best result for the PSCs based on PT derivatives up to date. In comparison with P3HT, by blending with ITIC, the alkoxycarbonyl-functionalized PDCBT can form better nanoscaled morphology so that less geminate and nongeminate charge recombinations can be achieved, thus leading to a better device performance. Since the PT polymers possess merits of easy synthesis and good stability, our study paves a promising way for highly efficient and low-cost PSCs for practical applications.
